00:11:05,164 --> 00:11:08,246
[SPEAKER_02]: My colleague asked about a fellow, he said, we need a pitcher.

206
00:11:08,286 --> 00:11:09,467
[SPEAKER_02]: Who is the good pitcher out here?

207
00:11:09,508 --> 00:11:10,649
[SPEAKER_02]: He said, we'll get Kowalewski.

208
00:11:10,669 --> 00:11:11,729
[SPEAKER_02]: He looks like the best.

209
00:11:12,850 --> 00:11:19,757
[SPEAKER_02]: So they informed me to Los Angeles if I'd come up and pitch for them.

210
00:11:20,918 --> 00:11:27,483
[SPEAKER_02]: I said, of course I'll come up and pitch for you, because I'm going to Los Angeles or San Francisco anyhow, because the World's Fair was on.

211
00:11:27,503 --> 00:11:28,644
[SPEAKER_02]: See, it was after the season.

212
00:11:29,185 --> 00:11:31,547
[SPEAKER_02]: I said, well, you're too old to make my expense anyhow.

213
00:11:33,259 --> 00:11:38,722
[SPEAKER_02]: When we started the game, Oscar Stannis catched me.

214
00:11:39,322 --> 00:11:43,945
[SPEAKER_02]: After a football, a fat ball, a slow ball, he gave me a spitter.

215
00:11:44,085 --> 00:11:46,206
[SPEAKER_02]: I threw a spitter.

216
00:11:48,527 --> 00:11:53,350
[SPEAKER_02]: He hit his glove in the end, and he walked out to me, and he said, be careful.

217
00:11:53,370 --> 00:11:55,291
[SPEAKER_02]: I said, what's the matter?

218
00:11:55,691 --> 00:11:56,511
[SPEAKER_02]: I missed a sign?

219
00:11:56,551 --> 00:11:57,212
[SPEAKER_02]: He said, no.

220
00:11:58,392 --> 00:11:59,593
[SPEAKER_02]: So from that time on,

221
00:12:00,862 --> 00:12:03,603
[SPEAKER_02]: Every ball player in the American National League wanted me.

222
00:12:03,663 --> 00:12:05,804
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, with all the different kind of ball players there.

223
00:12:05,824 --> 00:12:08,325
[SPEAKER_02]: When they seen what I had, now they couldn't even hit it.

224
00:12:08,885 --> 00:12:10,745
[SPEAKER_02]: That's how good I was.

225
00:12:10,785 --> 00:12:11,786
[SPEAKER_03]: Who was Stanage with?

226
00:12:11,806 --> 00:12:12,526
[SPEAKER_03]: Which team then?

227
00:12:12,686 --> 00:12:13,486
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, Detroit.

228
00:12:13,506 --> 00:12:14,987
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, he was Detroit.

229
00:12:16,107 --> 00:12:17,388
[SPEAKER_03]: But how'd Cleveland get you then?

230
00:12:17,748 --> 00:12:19,648
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, Cleveland owned Portland.

231
00:12:21,949 --> 00:12:25,410
[SPEAKER_03]: So Cleveland... Did his fellow McReady run Portland?

232
00:12:25,850 --> 00:12:26,451
[SPEAKER_02]: McReady, yeah.

233
00:12:26,531 --> 00:12:28,131
[SPEAKER_02]: Walter McReady.

234
00:12:28,171 --> 00:12:30,372
[SPEAKER_03]: Did he have anything to do with Cleveland?

235
00:12:30,873 --> 00:12:38,935
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, they had a contract between both, I think they did.

236
00:12:40,575 --> 00:12:48,737
[SPEAKER_02]: Anyhow, we're in New Orleans, see, I'm with Cleveland, and we're playing New York, I believe.

237
00:12:50,957 --> 00:12:57,798
[SPEAKER_02]: And we were in a hardball club, and the New Orleans hardball club was the ones that fought to play, you see.

238
00:12:58,599 --> 00:13:00,619
[SPEAKER_02]: And Jack Dobbs, Jack Dobbs,

239
00:13:01,448 --> 00:13:02,409
[SPEAKER_02]: He needed a pitch.

240
00:13:02,449 --> 00:13:03,809
[SPEAKER_02]: He came on and asked Lee Foley.

241
00:13:03,829 --> 00:13:06,851
[SPEAKER_02]: He says, Lee, how about on the Kowalewski lane?

242
00:13:07,972 --> 00:13:08,832
[SPEAKER_02]: So Lee asked.

243
00:13:08,972 --> 00:13:09,753
[SPEAKER_02]: I said, sure.

244
00:13:09,773 --> 00:13:11,414
[SPEAKER_02]: I'll go on the pitch.

245
00:13:11,694 --> 00:13:14,455
[SPEAKER_02]: Here's one I thought, well, here's one I'm going to be left in the morning.

246
00:13:15,796 --> 00:13:20,539
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, I went in there for five innings, thinking that we'd hit a ball out of the infield.

247
00:13:20,679 --> 00:13:23,921
[SPEAKER_02]: So Lee walked over and said, that's enough for him.

248
00:13:24,961 --> 00:13:27,983
[SPEAKER_02]: So Jimmy and Doug, the way they listened to them stories, I heard, see?

249
00:13:28,400 --> 00:13:31,883
[SPEAKER_02]: So Johnny Depp said, you leave Kowalewski here, I'm like a good pitcher.

250
00:13:32,083 --> 00:13:34,485
[SPEAKER_02]: He said, he's a good pitcher indeed.

251
00:13:34,585 --> 00:13:38,167
[SPEAKER_03]: He was a New Orleans manager, or?

252
00:13:38,187 --> 00:13:38,708
[SPEAKER_02]: He managed.

253
00:13:39,188 --> 00:13:43,211
[SPEAKER_03]: New Orleans, yeah.

254
00:13:43,251 --> 00:13:46,293
[SPEAKER_03]: How was your last year, how was your year in Portland there in 1915?

255
00:13:46,333 --> 00:13:47,054
[SPEAKER_03]: How'd you do there?

256
00:13:47,654 --> 00:13:48,915
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, listen.

257
00:13:49,656 --> 00:13:53,699
[SPEAKER_02]: I must have been in about 70 or 80 balls.

258
00:13:53,739 --> 00:13:56,621
[SPEAKER_02]: I only went 17, but I must have been in 80 balls.

259
00:13:57,591 --> 00:14:01,274
[SPEAKER_02]: But I don't know how many I went for big and bottom and lush.

260
00:14:01,354 --> 00:14:07,280
[SPEAKER_02]: And there were three pitchers there to go about four or five in, and they were done, see?

261
00:14:07,340 --> 00:14:08,641
[SPEAKER_02]: So they'd say, come on, it's been me.

262
00:14:08,761 --> 00:14:09,702
[SPEAKER_03]: Did your arm get tired?

263
00:14:10,282 --> 00:14:10,462
[SPEAKER_03]: Huh?

264
00:14:10,642 --> 00:14:11,363
[SPEAKER_03]: Did you get tired?

265
00:14:12,264 --> 00:14:12,704
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't know.

266
00:14:12,764 --> 00:14:14,045
[SPEAKER_03]: I've never seen it get tired.

267
00:14:15,487 --> 00:14:16,067
[SPEAKER_03]: Rubber arm.

268
00:14:17,028 --> 00:14:18,870
[SPEAKER_02]: I've never had a sore arm.

269
00:14:20,351 --> 00:14:24,274
[SPEAKER_02]: And I never, all I ever got was two days rest in the Olympic League.

270
00:14:25,148 --> 00:14:28,949
[SPEAKER_02]: That's all I wanted, because if I had three or four days, I'm too wild.

271
00:14:29,409 --> 00:14:31,229
[SPEAKER_02]: I go out there, I'm too fast, see?

272
00:14:32,189 --> 00:14:34,830
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm trying to throw a bomb.

273
00:14:35,230 --> 00:14:40,731
[SPEAKER_02]: Or, in other words, I just throw half the balls.

274
00:14:40,791 --> 00:14:47,572
[SPEAKER_02]: Why should I throw six, seven, nine balls of powder, and I can throw two or three?

275
00:14:47,612 --> 00:14:49,512
[SPEAKER_02]: That's how I save myself all the time.

276
00:14:50,433 --> 00:14:52,773
[SPEAKER_03]: I interviewed Leroy Parmalee yesterday.

277
00:14:53,498 --> 00:14:55,819
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, just the reverse, he couldn't get the ball over the plate.

278
00:14:56,279 --> 00:14:57,839
[SPEAKER_03]: And that was why his career was so short.

279
00:14:58,380 --> 00:14:59,420
[SPEAKER_03]: He could throw hard, I guess.

280
00:14:59,700 --> 00:15:04,161
[SPEAKER_02]: And at that time, listen, you had to get that ball over because everybody choked up.

281
00:15:05,322 --> 00:15:05,602
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

282
00:15:06,182 --> 00:15:08,083
[SPEAKER_02]: You'd take the righty, he'd stand up.

283
00:15:08,103 --> 00:15:09,723
[SPEAKER_02]: Take a fellow with control today.

284
00:15:11,624 --> 00:15:13,464
[SPEAKER_02]: He had to make the best pitch in the world.

285
00:15:13,584 --> 00:15:17,425
[SPEAKER_02]: If he has control, one of them fellows will stand back there and end the bat.

286
00:15:19,226 --> 00:15:22,207
[SPEAKER_03]: How many spitball pitchers were there in the American League?

287
00:15:23,006 --> 00:15:24,307
[SPEAKER_02]: Very many.

288
00:15:25,848 --> 00:15:26,208
[SPEAKER_02]: Me.

289
00:15:26,688 --> 00:15:27,489
[SPEAKER_02]: Carl Mays.

290
00:15:28,009 --> 00:15:29,250
[SPEAKER_02]: No, Mays wasn't.

291
00:15:29,490 --> 00:15:30,170
[SPEAKER_03]: Mays wasn't.

292
00:15:30,490 --> 00:15:31,271
[SPEAKER_03]: Carl Mays.

293
00:15:31,291 --> 00:15:34,192
[SPEAKER_02]: There was about five, I guess.

294
00:15:34,933 --> 00:15:38,955
[SPEAKER_03]: What did you think when they abolished the... Of course, it didn't affect you.

295
00:15:38,975 --> 00:15:40,196
[SPEAKER_02]: No, it wouldn't affect me.

296
00:15:40,236 --> 00:15:43,558
[SPEAKER_03]: But what did you people that threw the spitball, what did you think about that?

297
00:15:43,598 --> 00:15:44,938
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, I don't know.

298
00:15:45,599 --> 00:15:47,600
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't see why they ever took it out.

299
00:15:49,661 --> 00:15:51,422
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what I say, the roots.

300
00:15:52,010 --> 00:15:54,790
[SPEAKER_02]: And Ruth had to hit it in, all that stuff.

301
00:15:55,111 --> 00:15:59,871
[SPEAKER_02]: A black ball, spit ball, emery ball, knuckle ball, shine ball.

302
00:15:59,911 --> 00:16:00,912
[SPEAKER_02]: He had to hit it in.

303
00:16:00,932 --> 00:16:05,432
[SPEAKER_02]: Then still, he'd keep a ball in there for maybe two or three games before he'd take it out.

304
00:16:06,933 --> 00:16:10,193
[SPEAKER_03]: Did you like to use a ball that was pretty well worn?

305
00:16:10,213 --> 00:16:11,173
[SPEAKER_03]: Was that easier to...

306
00:16:11,273 --> 00:16:12,774
[SPEAKER_02]: It didn't make any difference, no.

307
00:16:13,054 --> 00:16:13,794
[SPEAKER_02]: It didn't make any difference.

308
00:16:14,194 --> 00:16:20,975
[SPEAKER_02]: What I used to watch, if I got a ball and there was a scratch on it, you could make that ball sail any way you wanted to make it sail.

309
00:16:21,673 --> 00:16:25,615
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, at the tray, I see the, the minute they look at the ball, they throw it out.

310
00:16:28,736 --> 00:16:29,677
[SPEAKER_00]: What's a black ball?

311
00:16:30,057 --> 00:16:30,257
[SPEAKER_02]: Huh?

312
00:16:30,597 --> 00:16:31,777
[SPEAKER_00]: What's a black ball?

313
00:16:33,878 --> 00:16:37,120
[SPEAKER_02]: A lot of liquid in the back of your hand.

314
00:16:38,540 --> 00:16:39,721
[SPEAKER_00]: I just wondered.

315
00:16:40,621 --> 00:16:41,762
[SPEAKER_02]: They're black and rubber.

316
00:16:41,962 --> 00:16:43,142
[SPEAKER_02]: They're black and rubber.

317
00:16:43,162 --> 00:16:46,464
[SPEAKER_03]: The emery balls where you had a little piece of sandpaper in your glove, is that right?

318
00:16:46,484 --> 00:16:47,444
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, they'd have in the belt.

319
00:16:47,584 --> 00:16:47,744
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh.

320
00:16:48,385 --> 00:16:49,045
[SPEAKER_02]: Just scratch it.

321
00:16:51,488 --> 00:16:52,909
[SPEAKER_02]: There's always just one thing.

322
00:16:53,169 --> 00:16:56,370
[SPEAKER_02]: The shine ball, you fill the seams with paracene, see?

323
00:16:56,390 --> 00:16:57,451
[SPEAKER_02]: A little dirt on there.

324
00:16:58,571 --> 00:17:00,792
[SPEAKER_02]: And you shine the other part up.

325
00:17:01,752 --> 00:17:02,933
[SPEAKER_02]: All balance of the ball, see?

326
00:17:03,173 --> 00:17:07,635
[SPEAKER_02]: And if you know how a ball is all balanced, you can make a curve any way you want to make a curve.

327
00:17:08,695 --> 00:17:14,378
[SPEAKER_02]: Just like, say, you see a scratch on a ball, you can make that ball go any way you want to make it.

328
00:17:15,758 --> 00:17:17,199
[SPEAKER_02]: Seems funny, but you can do it.

329
00:17:21,143 --> 00:17:22,324
[SPEAKER_01]: Tricks and trades.

330
00:17:24,125 --> 00:17:27,167
[SPEAKER_03]: Did your catchers have much trouble catching your spinner?

331
00:17:27,247 --> 00:17:31,049
[SPEAKER_03]: Any of your catchers, or it's a hard pitch to catch?

332
00:17:31,389 --> 00:17:33,730
[SPEAKER_02]: I seen Stephen Heeler one time in Washington.

333
00:17:35,351 --> 00:17:38,553
[SPEAKER_02]: Sam Rice gets up and gets a triple off me, first man.

334
00:17:40,054 --> 00:17:44,317
[SPEAKER_02]: So I strike the next three men out, and Stephen Heeler never held a ball in his hand.

335
00:17:44,337 --> 00:17:45,757
[SPEAKER_02]: I hit him all over.

336
00:17:47,338 --> 00:17:50,180
[SPEAKER_02]: Second hands, the first fellow gets up and gets a double off me.

337
00:17:51,940 --> 00:17:55,241
[SPEAKER_02]: I strike the next two out, and the other fellow popped out.

338
00:17:55,261 --> 00:18:00,502
[SPEAKER_02]: And this speaker came in, and he says, hell, he said, if you can't catch him, he says, I'll put somebody else in there, catch him.

339
00:18:01,483 --> 00:18:05,004
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's how good I was that day.

340
00:18:05,084 --> 00:18:09,885
[SPEAKER_02]: If I had to put the speed on everything, I could pick that ball any way I wanted to.

341
00:18:11,205 --> 00:18:12,566
[SPEAKER_03]: How often did you throw the spitter?

342
00:18:13,366 --> 00:18:16,067
[SPEAKER_02]: Not very often, but you always look for it.

343
00:18:16,187 --> 00:18:18,167
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, that was part of the value of it.

344
00:18:18,247 --> 00:18:20,768
[SPEAKER_02]: If you're looking for a spitball, and you're throwing fastball,

345
00:18:21,381 --> 00:18:22,481
[SPEAKER_02]: You got them all balanced.

346
00:18:22,521 --> 00:18:22,742
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

347
00:18:23,902 --> 00:18:26,423
[SPEAKER_03]: What would the spitball do that made it so hard to hit?

348
00:18:27,644 --> 00:18:29,044
[SPEAKER_03]: Or was it the fact that...

349
00:18:29,304 --> 00:18:32,325
[SPEAKER_02]: Listen, spitball is no different than a knuckleball.

350
00:18:32,345 --> 00:18:36,367
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, you've seen what a knuckleball... You know a good knuckleball, you can't catch.

351
00:18:37,227 --> 00:18:37,468
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

352
00:18:38,308 --> 00:18:39,508
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the same with spitball.

353
00:18:39,668 --> 00:18:41,229
[SPEAKER_02]: I reverse the ball, see?

354
00:18:43,110 --> 00:18:44,150
[SPEAKER_02]: When I let go, you...

355
00:18:44,490 --> 00:18:47,052
[SPEAKER_02]: If I throw a ball, it'll naturally go out this way.

356
00:18:47,632 --> 00:18:50,673
[SPEAKER_02]: I throw a ball, I held it back with my thumb, and it went off its way.

357
00:18:51,219 --> 00:18:51,959
[SPEAKER_02]: I just reversed.

358
00:18:56,182 --> 00:19:01,724
[SPEAKER_03]: Did you have, did anybody teach you the fine art of pitching?

359
00:19:01,945 --> 00:19:05,426
[SPEAKER_03]: Or as you came up, like Connie Mack, would he give you a few tips?

360
00:19:05,606 --> 00:19:08,688
[SPEAKER_02]: I had four brothers, all ballplayers.

361
00:19:09,989 --> 00:19:11,289
[SPEAKER_02]: Harry, he was a Detroit.

362
00:19:11,670 --> 00:19:13,891
[SPEAKER_02]: Frank, he joined the Union League in Philadelphia.

363
00:19:13,911 --> 00:19:16,212
[SPEAKER_02]: He was a pitcher too, but he got movement in and went out.

364
00:19:16,980 --> 00:19:19,982
[SPEAKER_02]: John, he was tried out with Philadelphia, and he was with Lancaster.

365
00:19:20,122 --> 00:19:22,723
[SPEAKER_02]: And Jake, the older brother, well, he was a pitcher, too.

366
00:19:22,783 --> 00:19:25,325
[SPEAKER_02]: I didn't know much about him, because, you know, I was too young then.

367
00:19:26,325 --> 00:19:30,507
[SPEAKER_02]: But there's no one that never asked me or never told me how to do this, how to do that.

368
00:19:30,848 --> 00:19:31,788
[SPEAKER_02]: I picked myself.

369
00:19:31,948 --> 00:19:32,829
[SPEAKER_03]: There were five brothers?

370
00:19:33,089 --> 00:19:33,329
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

371
00:19:33,349 --> 00:19:33,929
[SPEAKER_03]: Five of you.

372
00:19:35,010 --> 00:19:36,871
[SPEAKER_03]: What did your parents think about this?

373
00:19:36,891 --> 00:19:39,012
[SPEAKER_03]: Did they like to have you play ball, or did they not really?

374
00:19:39,032 --> 00:19:42,074
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, we used to get held because we'd tear up too many shoes.

375
00:19:42,414 --> 00:19:44,115
[SPEAKER_03]: Tear up too many shoes?

376
00:19:44,155 --> 00:19:44,575
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

377
00:19:46,684 --> 00:19:51,248
[SPEAKER_02]: There wasn't much money at that time, they couldn't afford buying shoes all the time.

378
00:19:51,308 --> 00:19:55,271
[SPEAKER_03]: Did your parents marry here, or did they come from... No, they married here.

379
00:19:55,351 --> 00:19:57,012
[SPEAKER_03]: Where'd they come from, and when?

380
00:19:57,292 --> 00:19:57,672
[SPEAKER_03]: Poland.

381
00:19:57,973 --> 00:19:59,254
[SPEAKER_03]: When did they come over, you remember?

382
00:19:59,494 --> 00:20:00,294
[SPEAKER_02]: No, I couldn't.

383
00:20:01,455 --> 00:20:02,916
[SPEAKER_02]: That's too far back for me.

384
00:20:03,677 --> 00:20:05,158
[SPEAKER_03]: But they were married before they came here.

385
00:20:05,178 --> 00:20:05,378
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

386
00:20:05,438 --> 00:20:06,799
[SPEAKER_02]: Were all you boys born here?

387
00:20:07,100 --> 00:20:09,161
[SPEAKER_02]: All born, all born in the Fiddler.

388
00:20:09,902 --> 00:20:13,785
[SPEAKER_02]: Only a little bit of a patch outside of Smokin', but a dozen homes here.

389
00:20:16,823 --> 00:20:17,564
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the name of the...

390
00:20:18,444 --> 00:20:20,065
[SPEAKER_02]: There was only about 12 homes there.

391
00:20:20,085 --> 00:20:21,846
[SPEAKER_02]: There was a breaker there, see?

392
00:20:22,107 --> 00:20:23,247
[SPEAKER_02]: A coal mine breaker there.

393
00:20:23,327 --> 00:20:25,749
[SPEAKER_02]: It was a fiddler, then they called his house the fiddler.

394
00:20:25,909 --> 00:20:27,750
[SPEAKER_03]: Is that how your parents happened to settle there?

395
00:20:27,790 --> 00:20:29,031
[SPEAKER_03]: Because of the coal mines?

396
00:20:29,091 --> 00:20:29,712
[SPEAKER_03]: I think so, yeah.

397
00:20:29,772 --> 00:20:33,374
[SPEAKER_03]: I thought that was largely Irish.

398
00:20:34,375 --> 00:20:35,415
[SPEAKER_03]: Weren't there a lot of Irish there?

399
00:20:35,455 --> 00:20:37,757
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, the Irish is up in Locust Gap.

400
00:20:38,277 --> 00:20:39,138
[SPEAKER_02]: That's a little far too.

401
00:20:40,219 --> 00:20:42,000
[SPEAKER_02]: Were there many... That was all Irish.

402
00:20:42,240 --> 00:20:44,261
[SPEAKER_03]: Were there many Polish people in your community?

403
00:20:44,441 --> 00:20:45,302
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh yeah, quite a few.

404
00:20:46,317 --> 00:20:47,558
[SPEAKER_03]: Were you from this area at all?

405
00:20:48,518 --> 00:20:49,338
[SPEAKER_01]: From Smokin.

406
00:20:49,659 --> 00:20:50,219
[SPEAKER_03]: Were you?

407
00:20:50,239 --> 00:20:50,539
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes.

