976
00:48:33,435 --> 00:48:36,457
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't know why I did, but my arm was never sore.

977
00:48:38,110 --> 00:48:45,093
[SPEAKER_02]: I got tired, you know what I mean?

978
00:48:46,213 --> 00:48:47,494
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why you're in the Hall of Fame.

979
00:48:48,554 --> 00:48:57,718
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, that's quite a record there.

980
00:48:59,878 --> 00:49:04,000
[SPEAKER_03]: When did you first hear about being elected to the Hall of Fame?

981
00:49:05,282 --> 00:49:07,883
[SPEAKER_03]: Mrs. O'Connor, you asked her when she came in here.

982
00:49:08,063 --> 00:49:10,144
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, Mrs. Haynes told us that, too.

983
00:49:10,244 --> 00:49:14,025
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, she was the one that gave the story.

984
00:49:14,045 --> 00:49:15,966
[SPEAKER_02]: She gave you that story.

985
00:49:20,507 --> 00:49:22,088
[SPEAKER_00]: Do your boys live here in town?

986
00:49:22,788 --> 00:49:23,628
[SPEAKER_00]: Do your boys live here?

987
00:49:23,648 --> 00:49:25,329
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, he lives here in Western Avenue.

988
00:49:27,229 --> 00:49:31,511
[SPEAKER_02]: He's been with Sears, you know, for 28 years, taking care of our television down there.

989
00:49:33,225 --> 00:49:36,246
[SPEAKER_03]: Tell me, how did you happen to relocate here in South Bend?

990
00:49:37,026 --> 00:49:40,987
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, they had an amateur ball club here, and they had a good...

991
00:49:42,987 --> 00:49:45,247
[SPEAKER_03]: They offered me a good salary, and I came out here.

992
00:49:45,307 --> 00:49:46,888
[SPEAKER_03]: An amateur club offered you a salary?

993
00:49:46,908 --> 00:49:47,508
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah.

994
00:49:48,008 --> 00:49:50,909
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, if I was going to name a bachelor or a runner, it wasn't an amateur.

995
00:49:50,929 --> 00:49:51,569
[SPEAKER_02]: He had a...

996
00:49:51,589 --> 00:49:53,129
[SPEAKER_02]: He had a...

997
00:49:53,529 --> 00:49:58,050
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't know what he liked ball for, no league or anything, just... just a boring summer.

998
00:49:59,610 --> 00:49:59,950
[SPEAKER_02]: So...

999
00:50:00,451 --> 00:50:01,051
[SPEAKER_02]: When was this?

1000
00:50:01,791 --> 00:50:02,331
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, my...

1001
00:50:03,444 --> 00:50:04,784
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, thank you.

1002
00:50:04,905 --> 00:50:07,065
[SPEAKER_02]: After I was through with Washington.

1003
00:50:07,545 --> 00:50:07,985
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, I see.

1004
00:50:08,005 --> 00:50:09,346
[SPEAKER_03]: So you came here to play baseball?

1005
00:50:09,646 --> 00:50:09,926
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

1006
00:50:10,266 --> 00:50:11,487
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know if you want a glass.

1007
00:50:11,647 --> 00:50:12,287
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, that's fine.

1008
00:50:12,307 --> 00:50:13,547
[SPEAKER_03]: Thank you so much.

1009
00:50:15,128 --> 00:50:16,288
[SPEAKER_01]: You want a shot, Pop?

1010
00:50:16,908 --> 00:50:19,069
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, you know I want a shot.

1011
00:50:19,409 --> 00:50:20,649
[SPEAKER_01]: And you didn't bring him one.

1012
00:50:20,669 --> 00:50:21,430
[SPEAKER_00]: Shame on you.

1013
00:50:22,990 --> 00:50:29,092
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, well, have you lived in Shamokin all these years?

1014
00:50:30,252 --> 00:50:31,993
[SPEAKER_02]: No, I come right out here in 28.

1015
00:50:34,714 --> 00:50:37,456
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean, up until then, you went back to Shamokin every year?

1016
00:50:38,397 --> 00:50:38,897
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, yeah.

1017
00:50:39,157 --> 00:50:40,318
[SPEAKER_02]: Up to that time, yeah.

1018
00:50:40,438 --> 00:50:44,320
[SPEAKER_03]: Your home was there until 28 or 29 you came here.

1019
00:50:45,621 --> 00:50:47,843
[SPEAKER_03]: I see.

1020
00:50:47,863 --> 00:50:50,324
[SPEAKER_03]: What did you do then, after you played ball here?

1021
00:50:50,764 --> 00:50:54,407
[SPEAKER_03]: Did you do anything else?

1022
00:50:54,467 --> 00:50:54,687
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

1023
00:50:54,807 --> 00:50:56,068
[SPEAKER_02]: I had a filling station here.

1024
00:50:59,250 --> 00:51:00,551
[SPEAKER_02]: A filling station?

1025
00:51:01,591 --> 00:51:02,452
[SPEAKER_02]: These darn things.

1026
00:51:03,542 --> 00:51:06,504
[SPEAKER_02]: And depression-wise, I couldn't even let it go.

1027
00:51:06,524 --> 00:51:10,126
[SPEAKER_02]: There was no gasoline to buy or anything else, and I had to get rid of that.

1028
00:51:11,106 --> 00:51:13,668
[SPEAKER_02]: So I just laid it on since that time.

1029
00:51:15,449 --> 00:51:16,589
[SPEAKER_02]: I didn't do nothing since.

1030
00:51:19,511 --> 00:51:21,172
[SPEAKER_03]: Have you had this house since then?

1031
00:51:21,272 --> 00:51:22,673
[SPEAKER_03]: Have you always been in this house here?

1032
