1435
01:13:14,652 --> 01:13:22,794
[SPEAKER_02]: I think that room pretty near the scoreboard.

1436
01:13:30,557 --> 01:13:32,457
[SPEAKER_03]: Anybody ever hit the scoreboard you ever saw?

1437
01:13:33,157 --> 01:13:34,998
[SPEAKER_03]: I never seen nobody hit the scoreboard.

1438
01:13:35,998 --> 01:13:36,958
[SPEAKER_03]: That's a long distance.

1439
01:13:43,731 --> 01:13:45,392
[SPEAKER_03]: He'd be the best ballplayer you ever saw.

1440
01:13:46,293 --> 01:13:47,313
[SPEAKER_03]: Anybody stand out?

1441
01:13:47,333 --> 01:13:49,275
[SPEAKER_02]: No, they're all good ballplayers.

1442
01:13:49,595 --> 01:13:51,156
[SPEAKER_03]: Cobb stand out above Ruth?

1443
01:13:51,196 --> 01:13:53,457
[SPEAKER_02]: They're all good ballplayers.

1444
01:13:56,359 --> 01:14:01,543
[SPEAKER_02]: Cobb, he would, they probably better outfield him.

1445
01:14:01,563 --> 01:14:05,625
[SPEAKER_02]: Cobb is fast and all, and a good hitter, and that's what would overcome the outfield.

1446
01:14:05,645 --> 01:14:08,027
[SPEAKER_03]: Did Cobb try to hurt people on his flight?

1447
01:14:08,047 --> 01:14:11,869
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't think he did, no.

1448
01:14:11,929 --> 01:14:12,750
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't think he'd hurt anybody.

1449
01:14:13,207 --> 01:14:14,948
[SPEAKER_02]: I seen him one time in Cleveland.

1450
01:14:16,368 --> 01:14:18,549
[SPEAKER_02]: He come home and Steve O'Neal rocked him off.

1451
01:14:19,229 --> 01:14:23,090
[SPEAKER_02]: And he told Steve, he said, Steve, never stand in front of me again like that.

1452
01:14:23,170 --> 01:14:26,391
[SPEAKER_02]: He said, and I don't want to come under my spikes high.

1453
01:14:27,631 --> 01:14:28,532
[SPEAKER_02]: The same game.

1454
01:14:29,932 --> 01:14:39,055
[SPEAKER_02]: He come in the same way and held it, and boy, hit spikes, hit Steve in the chest, and see how the green get patched up.

1455
01:14:40,491 --> 01:14:43,272
[SPEAKER_02]: But listen, there weren't one word said between both of them.

1456
01:14:43,512 --> 01:14:44,493
[SPEAKER_02]: He told them, didn't he?

1457
01:14:44,513 --> 01:14:44,813
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

1458
01:14:45,553 --> 01:14:47,674
[SPEAKER_02]: All he said, give me one part of the plate.

1459
01:14:49,955 --> 01:14:52,136
[SPEAKER_02]: That's all he wanted, one part of the plate.

1460
01:14:52,616 --> 01:14:55,737
[SPEAKER_03]: Of course, the baseline belonged to the runner, and he was going to take that.

1461
01:14:55,757 --> 01:14:58,638
[SPEAKER_02]: But you say, well, he's catchy.

1462
01:14:59,119 --> 01:15:00,079
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the plate.

1463
01:15:00,259 --> 01:15:01,119
[SPEAKER_02]: He'll come to the plate.

1464
01:15:01,139 --> 01:15:02,360
[SPEAKER_02]: You can get in there.

1465
01:15:02,760 --> 01:15:05,161
[SPEAKER_02]: But what are you going to do?

1466
01:15:05,941 --> 01:15:06,582
[SPEAKER_02]: Not you and me.

1467
01:15:06,662 --> 01:15:09,863
[SPEAKER_02]: I'd jump both feet right in his chest myself.

1468
01:15:19,371 --> 01:15:21,913
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, and I think they all play for the love of the game.

1469
01:15:23,054 --> 01:15:26,257
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, that's what seems to come through talking to the men.

1470
01:15:26,937 --> 01:15:34,343
[SPEAKER_03]: I wanted to ask you, what was life like being the wife of a great ball player?

1471
01:15:34,363 --> 01:15:40,748
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, anytime he felt like going hunting, I never stood in his way.

1472
01:15:43,350 --> 01:15:44,011
[SPEAKER_01]: That's true.

1473
01:15:46,073 --> 01:15:46,413
[SPEAKER_01]: Never.

1474
01:15:47,167 --> 01:15:50,928
[SPEAKER_01]: And one thing he did say before he married me, he says, you know, I tune.

1475
01:15:51,288 --> 01:15:54,289
[SPEAKER_01]: He says, don't try to quit.

1476
01:15:54,750 --> 01:15:55,350
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't quit.

1477
01:15:55,490 --> 01:15:56,570
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't make me quit.

1478
01:15:56,590 --> 01:15:58,611
[SPEAKER_03]: That didn't last very long, though, did it?

1479
01:16:00,171 --> 01:16:00,372
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh.

1480
01:16:02,452 --> 01:16:03,493
[SPEAKER_01]: He tuned.

1481
01:16:03,693 --> 01:16:04,693
[SPEAKER_01]: He didn't quit.

1482
01:16:05,373 --> 01:16:06,574
[SPEAKER_01]: I never tell him to.

1483
01:16:07,174 --> 01:16:07,434
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

1484
01:16:08,074 --> 01:16:10,615
[SPEAKER_01]: He never stopped me from anything.

1485
01:16:10,695 --> 01:16:12,116
[SPEAKER_01]: Why should I stop him?

1486
01:16:13,636 --> 01:16:15,257
[SPEAKER_01]: He says, you can go to dances.

1487
01:16:15,317 --> 01:16:16,397
[SPEAKER_01]: He's no dancer.

1488
01:16:17,826 --> 01:16:19,547
[SPEAKER_01]: I used to go with my friends.

1489
01:16:21,508 --> 01:16:26,851
[SPEAKER_01]: Ain't that true, Colman?

1490
01:16:29,793 --> 01:16:33,535
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, you don't go to see Notre Dame football though, huh?

1491
01:16:33,656 --> 01:16:34,576
[SPEAKER_02]: I'd rather go fishing.

1492
01:16:35,377 --> 01:16:36,037
[SPEAKER_02]: That's true.

1493
01:16:36,617 --> 01:16:37,298
[SPEAKER_02]: That's true.

1494
01:16:37,318 --> 01:16:38,158
[SPEAKER_02]: I enjoy fishing.

1495
01:16:45,680 --> 01:16:48,380
[SPEAKER_01]: You watch on TV sometimes.

1496
01:16:48,420 --> 01:16:50,661
[SPEAKER_02]: You go out there.

1497
01:16:50,881 --> 01:16:52,661
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't drive too much.

1498
01:16:52,681 --> 01:16:56,102
[SPEAKER_02]: You drive out there and all that traffic, and that traffic coming home.

1499
01:16:58,762 --> 01:17:01,043
[SPEAKER_01]: But he's been out at the games.

1500
01:17:01,223 --> 01:17:02,723
[SPEAKER_02]: I drive only at night.

1501
01:17:02,823 --> 01:17:03,743
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't drive at night.

1502
01:17:03,763 --> 01:17:07,524
[SPEAKER_02]: Because I know I'm not the driver I used to be.

1503
01:17:12,545 --> 01:17:13,145
[SPEAKER_01]: That's true.

1504
01:17:13,185 --> 01:17:15,345
[SPEAKER_01]: When you get older, things go wrong.

1505
01:17:16,035 --> 01:17:18,776
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not as strong, I don't think.

1506
01:17:18,936 --> 01:17:24,097
[SPEAKER_01]: You see too much danger now with these colored people.

1507
01:17:24,637 --> 01:17:25,797
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just no fun.

1508
01:17:29,258 --> 01:17:32,119
[SPEAKER_03]: What was it like up in Cooperstown for the ceremonies there?

1509
01:17:32,339 --> 01:17:34,339
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, wonderful, wonderful.

1510
01:17:34,779 --> 01:17:35,960
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I was nervous.

1511
01:17:36,020 --> 01:17:37,840
[SPEAKER_01]: That was my first experience.

1512
01:17:38,460 --> 01:17:39,180
[SPEAKER_01]: He got up.

1513
01:17:41,101 --> 01:17:42,361
[SPEAKER_01]: He couldn't get started.

1514
01:17:42,941 --> 01:17:45,642
[SPEAKER_01]: Good God, me sitting there wondering.

1515
01:17:46,960 --> 01:17:52,001
[SPEAKER_01]: So somebody come along, pat him on the back, and he started going, then he forgot where the period was.

1516
01:17:53,142 --> 01:17:59,983
[SPEAKER_01]: When he started, he forgot to quit.

1517
01:18:00,984 --> 01:18:04,264
[SPEAKER_01]: And he's no speech maker, but I'm not kidding.

1518
01:18:04,284 --> 01:18:07,865
[SPEAKER_02]: He told right away, he said, you know, I'm no speaker.

1519
01:18:16,002 --> 01:18:18,944
[SPEAKER_01]: And he says, by being a minor, too.

1520
01:18:19,345 --> 01:18:20,145
[SPEAKER_01]: He told that.

1521
01:18:20,605 --> 01:18:31,273
[SPEAKER_01]: And some people, when we were out at the table eating, there was two or three women come and congratulated him.

1522
01:18:31,313 --> 01:18:32,534
[SPEAKER_01]: But there was others came.

1523
01:18:32,694 --> 01:18:38,439
[SPEAKER_01]: But these three women, they must have had fathers that were minors.

1524
01:18:39,019 --> 01:18:40,620
[SPEAKER_01]: And I'm not kidding you.

1525
01:18:40,761 --> 01:18:42,041
[SPEAKER_01]: They even tried.

1526
01:18:50,126 --> 01:18:55,010
[SPEAKER_01]: One thing I was proud of, he didn't have no paper written.

1527
01:18:55,850 --> 01:18:57,431
[SPEAKER_01]: Stan Musial got up.

1528
01:18:57,471 --> 01:18:58,572
[SPEAKER_01]: He had papers.

1529
01:18:58,832 --> 01:19:02,975
[SPEAKER_01]: And I thought, boy, that's big.

1530
01:19:04,236 --> 01:19:05,077
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's true.

1531
01:19:05,697 --> 01:19:08,600
[SPEAKER_01]: He read off the papers.

1532
01:19:11,742 --> 01:19:13,823
[SPEAKER_00]: So have you gone back every year now?

1533
01:19:14,244 --> 01:19:16,125
[SPEAKER_01]: Every year so far, we've been going.

1534
01:19:16,620 --> 01:19:17,501
[SPEAKER_01]: Isn't that wonderful?

1535
01:19:17,801 --> 01:19:18,961
[SPEAKER_03]: Do you get flown up?

1536
01:19:19,001 --> 01:19:20,562
[SPEAKER_03]: They fly you up, huh?

1537
01:19:20,682 --> 01:19:21,122
[SPEAKER_01]: Mm-hmm.

1538
01:19:21,403 --> 01:19:21,703
[SPEAKER_01]: What?

1539
01:19:21,803 --> 01:19:24,604
[SPEAKER_03]: They fly you up to Utica or whatever?

1540
01:19:24,724 --> 01:19:25,124
[SPEAKER_01]: Utica.

1541
01:19:25,144 --> 01:19:26,545
[SPEAKER_03]: They have a car come and get you.

1542
01:19:26,705 --> 01:19:27,025
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

1543
01:19:27,306 --> 01:19:32,268
[SPEAKER_02]: Last year, my son and the wife had driven up there, see?

1544
01:19:32,888 --> 01:19:35,210
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, after the war, we'd come home with them, see?

1545
01:19:35,470 --> 01:19:39,632
[SPEAKER_02]: So we walked through Pennsylvania, my home town.

1546
01:19:39,832 --> 01:19:42,773
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, and he took them to the mountains.

1547
01:19:43,594 --> 01:19:45,555
[SPEAKER_02]: So I don't know if he's going to help this year or not.

1548
01:19:46,091 --> 01:19:48,692
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you don't know about this gas situation.

1549
01:19:49,092 --> 01:19:52,153
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't know from one day to another what you're going to hear.

1550
01:19:53,173 --> 01:19:56,774
[SPEAKER_02]: He even took me up in the mountains at our camps.

1551
01:19:58,014 --> 01:20:00,575
[SPEAKER_02]: Sure, where you used to be with Joe Wood.

1552
01:20:00,775 --> 01:20:03,576
[SPEAKER_03]: Do you think Joe Wood would mind being interviewed?

1553
01:20:05,056 --> 01:20:09,097
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, he might be, but... Oh, I don't see... You'd have to ask him.

1554
01:20:12,352 --> 01:20:13,913
[SPEAKER_02]: So I hope he gets in there.

1555
01:20:14,093 --> 01:20:15,434
[SPEAKER_02]: I hope he gets in.

1556
01:20:16,755 --> 01:20:18,777
[SPEAKER_02]: But they say you've got to be in there so many years.

1557
01:20:20,138 --> 01:20:24,621
[SPEAKER_03]: I wonder if I went up there with this tape recorder, do you think he'd object?

1558
01:20:24,781 --> 01:20:26,703
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I don't think.

1559
01:20:26,763 --> 01:20:29,425
[SPEAKER_01]: He did it for Larry.

1560
01:20:30,105 --> 01:20:30,886
[SPEAKER_01]: What's his name?

1561
01:20:30,946 --> 01:20:31,246
[SPEAKER_01]: Ritter?

1562
01:20:31,647 --> 01:20:31,987
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

1563
01:20:32,227 --> 01:20:32,928
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, is Ritter here?

1564
01:20:32,968 --> 01:20:34,369
[SPEAKER_01]: That's his picture there.

1565
01:20:34,989 --> 01:20:35,750
[SPEAKER_01]: He was here.

1566
01:20:35,930 --> 01:20:36,530
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah.

1567
01:20:36,811 --> 01:20:37,431
[SPEAKER_01]: That's him.

1568
01:20:37,491 --> 01:20:38,192
[SPEAKER_03]: Is that right?

1569
01:20:39,503 --> 01:20:40,624
[SPEAKER_01]: The glory of their time.

1570
01:20:40,644 --> 01:20:42,385
[SPEAKER_01]: But I wasn't home at the time.

1571
01:20:43,566 --> 01:21:00,957
[SPEAKER_01]: And another thing, last year was the first time that Larrick, so Coley, I guess he come over to Stan, he'd seen Stan, we shook hands with him, so Coley and him talked, but I'm with somebody else not paying any attention.

1572
01:21:01,478 --> 01:21:04,880
[SPEAKER_01]: So pretty soon this fella comes over and he says, can I sit down?

1573
01:21:04,940 --> 01:21:06,101
[SPEAKER_01]: I says, sure, you can.

1574
01:21:07,161 --> 01:21:08,082
[SPEAKER_01]: It was a soul talk.

1575
01:21:09,530 --> 01:21:12,451
[SPEAKER_01]: And he says, do you come here every year?

1576
01:21:12,591 --> 01:21:14,112
[SPEAKER_01]: I said, well, so far.

1577
01:21:15,752 --> 01:21:19,113
[SPEAKER_01]: And then he says, something about baseball.

1578
01:21:20,174 --> 01:21:21,354
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

1579
01:21:21,554 --> 01:21:24,515
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, he says, about the player.

1580
01:21:24,555 --> 01:21:28,657
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I says, I happen to be Mrs. Kowalewski.

1581
01:21:29,717 --> 01:21:34,039
[SPEAKER_01]: And then he says, I said, didn't you read about him?

1582
01:21:35,259 --> 01:21:37,440
[SPEAKER_01]: He says, no, I don't read the papers.

1583
01:21:40,657 --> 01:21:42,418
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, then I says, you don't know him.

1584
01:21:44,558 --> 01:21:48,959
[SPEAKER_01]: And he got scared for fear I'd open my big mouth and say something.

1585
01:21:50,700 --> 01:21:52,821
[SPEAKER_01]: You know how a woman gets rattled.

1586
01:21:54,061 --> 01:21:58,742
[SPEAKER_01]: So he said, he come over, and he says, you know who this is.

1587
01:21:58,822 --> 01:22:01,503
[SPEAKER_01]: And this guy is stepping aside of me all the time.

1588
01:22:02,263 --> 01:22:04,804
[SPEAKER_01]: And he's nice looking, a friendly person.

1589
01:22:06,524 --> 01:22:08,365
[SPEAKER_01]: So I didn't do much talking.

1590
01:22:09,397 --> 01:22:10,818
[SPEAKER_01]: He was asking questions.

1591
01:22:11,959 --> 01:22:16,543
[SPEAKER_01]: So pretty soon, Colby comes over and he says, he's laughing like a rascal.

1592
01:22:16,583 --> 01:22:18,425
[SPEAKER_01]: I wonder, what's he laughing at?

1593
01:22:19,806 --> 01:22:22,588
[SPEAKER_01]: So he says, do you know who this guy is?

1594
01:22:22,668 --> 01:22:24,650
[SPEAKER_01]: I says, no, I don't know who he is.

1595
01:22:25,410 --> 01:22:26,851
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not smiling, you know.

1596
01:22:27,512 --> 01:22:32,356
[SPEAKER_01]: Only I felt, ain't that funny, asking me that, and then he don't read the papers?

1597
01:22:34,178 --> 01:22:37,080
[SPEAKER_01]: So when he says, Larry Ritter,

1598
01:22:37,719 --> 01:22:41,381
[SPEAKER_01]: if that floor would have opened up by the melt room.

1599
01:22:42,382 --> 01:22:45,123
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, you don't know how I felt.

1600
01:22:47,985 --> 01:22:49,706
[SPEAKER_01]: So I apologized to him.

1601
01:22:49,726 --> 01:22:51,867
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, oh, you don't have to.

1602
01:22:52,167 --> 01:22:53,408
[SPEAKER_01]: And I kissed him.

1603
01:22:53,748 --> 01:22:57,831
[SPEAKER_01]: And oh my, he was, he sure, do you know him?

1604
01:22:58,231 --> 01:22:58,891
[SPEAKER_03]: No, never met him.

1605
01:22:59,272 --> 01:23:00,933
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, he's a friendly guy.

1606
01:23:02,013 --> 01:23:04,955
[SPEAKER_01]: And he teaches at New York University.

1607
01:23:06,006 --> 01:23:09,747
[SPEAKER_01]: But he changed his address now, but he always wrote to us.

1608
01:23:10,907 --> 01:23:14,688
[SPEAKER_01]: So one time, see, I was working at the time.

1609
01:23:14,748 --> 01:23:17,409
[SPEAKER_01]: Cody went down when they advertised that book.

1610
01:23:18,209 --> 01:23:19,949
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, Gloria, they're dying.

1611
01:23:21,210 --> 01:23:22,290
[SPEAKER_01]: So I didn't go.

1612
01:23:23,070 --> 01:23:27,971
[SPEAKER_01]: So Cody was down there, and he walked all over, dying for a chew.

1613
01:23:28,351 --> 01:23:29,292
[SPEAKER_01]: Couldn't buy any.

1614
01:23:30,512 --> 01:23:33,953
[SPEAKER_01]: So when Larry, he told me, when Larry come over, he says,

1615
01:23:35,372 --> 01:23:38,394
[SPEAKER_01]: place is this I Can Buy Chewtobacca.

1616
01:23:39,134 --> 01:23:44,457
[SPEAKER_01]: So Larry goes out and gets some Chewtobacca.

1617
01:23:44,477 --> 01:23:48,479
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, he's a grand guy.

1618
01:23:50,941 --> 01:23:52,321
[SPEAKER_01]: He wasn't there last year.

1619
01:23:52,341 --> 01:23:54,543
[SPEAKER_01]: A year before, that's when it was.

1620
01:23:55,283 --> 01:23:57,504
[SPEAKER_01]: And he always writes to us, Christmas.

1621
01:23:58,705 --> 01:24:00,046
[SPEAKER_01]: Always writes nice letters.

1622
01:24:01,387 --> 01:24:02,067
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, he did.

1623
01:24:03,522 --> 01:24:05,564
[SPEAKER_01]: And he's a nice-looking young man, too.

1624
01:24:05,624 --> 01:24:05,784
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

1625
01:24:07,265 --> 01:24:09,066
[SPEAKER_03]: It'd be great to be a nice-looking young man.

1626
01:24:12,028 --> 01:24:14,790
[SPEAKER_03]: Tell me, did you speak Polish at home?

1627
01:24:15,010 --> 01:24:15,551
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

1628
01:24:16,171 --> 01:24:17,332
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm so broken.

1629
01:24:17,692 --> 01:24:18,853
[SPEAKER_03]: Nobody understands.

1630
01:24:18,873 --> 01:24:20,274
[SPEAKER_01]: But at home, at church.

1631
01:24:20,354 --> 01:24:21,896
[SPEAKER_03]: At home, we're broken.

1632
01:24:21,996 --> 01:24:22,956
[SPEAKER_03]: Mostly in English.

1633
01:24:23,317 --> 01:24:24,177
[SPEAKER_03]: Do you speak Polish?

1634
01:24:24,377 --> 01:24:26,199
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, I can understand it, but I can't speak it.

1635
01:24:26,219 --> 01:24:26,899
[SPEAKER_02]: He chops it up.

1636
01:24:26,919 --> 01:24:28,080
[SPEAKER_02]: I understand it all.

1637
01:24:28,620 --> 01:24:29,941
[SPEAKER_03]: Did your parents speak it?

1638
01:24:30,122 --> 01:24:30,542
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm told.

1639
01:24:31,022 --> 01:24:32,262
[SPEAKER_03]: Were your parents speaking at home?

1640
01:24:32,442 --> 01:24:33,583
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, yeah, they spoke it.

1641
